{
  "summary": "Restricts the -bytespersigop configuration option at startup to the range [0, 21474] to keep sigop-adjusted vsize calculations within int32 limits. In addition, moves the standard transaction sigop limit check in MemPoolAccept::PreChecks to run before a transaction is staged in the mempool changeset. Adds functional test coverage for parameter boundary validation and sigop overflow cases.",
  "problem": "-bytespersigop is parsed as int64 and cast to unsigned int, allowing negative numbers to wrap and values of 2^32 or greater to truncate (e.g. 4294967296 silently zeroes the setting). High values cause sigop-adjusted transaction and package sizes to overflow int32_t, crashing debug builds on Assume assertions in fee rate and transaction graph calculations. Furthermore, because PreChecks staged transactions before verifying MAX_STANDARD_TX_SIGOPS_COST, standardness rejection occurred too late to prevent large-sigop transactions from triggering the same overflow during staging.",
  "evidence": [
    "Commit fac9c100c7 notes that with -bytespersigop above 21474, \"a package of 25 transactions at the limit aborts a debug build on Assume(virtual_bytes >= 0) in CFeeRate::GetFee; above 134217, so does a single transaction, on Assume(feerate.size > 0) in TxGraphImpl::AddTransaction.\"",
    "Commit 7d37a758b3 notes that even within the new limit at -bytespersigop=21474, an incoming transaction with 100,040 sigops results in 2,148,258,960 sigop-adjusted weight exceeding INT32_MAX, aborting debug builds on Assume(feerate.size > 0) during staging."
  ],
